{"name":"Silver Medal","hex":"#d6d6d6","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"silver medal","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A medal made of, or having the color of, silver, given as a prize for finishing in second place.","Finishing in second position, being a runner-up."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/silver_medal"}],"entries":[{"title":"Silver medal","blocks":[{"text":"A silver medal, in sports and other similar areas involving competition, is a medal made of, or plated with, silver awarded to the second-place finisher, or runner-up, of contests or competitions such as the Olympic Games, Commonwealth Games, etc. The outright winner receives a gold medal and the third place a bronze medal. More generally, silver is traditionally a metal sometimes used for all types of high-quality medals, including artistic ones."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/1896_Olympic_medal.jpg/330px-1896_Olympic_medal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_medal","wikidata":"Q847956"},{"title":"Silver Medal (Zoological Society of London)","blocks":[{"text":"The Silver Medal of the Zoological Society of London is \"Awarded to a Fellow of the Society or any other person for contributions to the understanding and appreciation of zoology, including such activities as public education in natural history, and wildlife conservation.\"\nIt was first awarded in 1847."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_Medal_(Zoological_Society_of_London)","wikidata":"Q7516191"},{"title":"Medal","blocks":[{"text":"A medal or medallion is a small portable artistic object, a thin disc, normally of metal, carrying a design, usually on both sides. They often have a commemorative purpose, and many are presented as awards. They may be intended to be worn, suspended from clothing or jewellery, although this has not always been the case. They may be struck by dies, as most coins are, or die-cast in a mould."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Portrait_medal_of_Cecilia_Gonzaga_%28obverse%29%3B_Innocence_and_a_Unicorn_in_a_Moonlit_Landscape_%28reverse%29_MET_DP-1241-015_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Portrait_medal_of_Cecilia_Gonzaga_%28obverse%29%3B_Innocence_and_a_Unicorn_in_a_Moonlit_Landscape_%28reverse%29_MET_DP-1241-015_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Medal","wikidata":"Q131647"},{"title":"Silver","blocks":[{"text":"Silver is a chemical element; it has symbol Ag and atomic number 47. A soft, white, lustrous transition metal, it exhibits the highest electrical conductivity, thermal conductivity and reflectivity of any metal. Silver is found in the Earth's crust in the pure, free elemental form, as an alloy with gold (electrum) and other metals, and in minerals such as acanthite, argentite and chlorargyrite. Most silver is recovered as a byproduct of lead–zinc, copper and gold mining and refining."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Silver_crystal.jpg/330px-Silver_crystal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver","wikidata":"Q1090"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Pisanello%2C_medaglia_di_cecilia_gonzaga.JPG/500px-Pisanello%2C_medaglia_di_cecilia_gonzaga.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Pisanello%2C_medaglia_di_cecilia_gonzaga.JPG/1280px-Pisanello%2C_medaglia_di_cecilia_gonzaga.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Pisanello%2C_medaglia_di_cecilia_gonzaga.JPG/500px-Pisanello%2C_medaglia_di_cecilia_gonzaga.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Reverse of the same medal, this copy with a suspension hole added later (inside a crescent moon in the design).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APisanello%2C_medaglia_di_cecilia_gonzaga.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Dupont_KBS-FRB%282%29.jpg/500px-Dupont_KBS-FRB%282%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Dupont_KBS-FRB%282%29.jpg/1280px-Dupont_KBS-FRB%282%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Dupont_KBS-FRB%282%29.jpg/500px-Dupont_KBS-FRB%282%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Medal depicting Herbert C. Hoover by Devreese Godefroi","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADupont_KBS-FRB(2).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Medals.jpg/500px-Medals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Medals.jpg/500px-Medals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Various prize medals with obverse designs, suspension rings and ribbons typical of medals intended to be draped over the head and hung from the neck","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMedals.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Peter_Fl%C3%B6tner_Vanitas_BNM.jpg/500px-Peter_Fl%C3%B6tner_Vanitas_BNM.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Peter_Fl%C3%B6tner_Vanitas_BNM.jpg/1280px-Peter_Fl%C3%B6tner_Vanitas_BNM.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb